                <head>140. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On September 26, Secretary Dulles met in New York with Foreign Secretary
                    Macmillan, who raised the question of Cyprus. According to the memorandum of
                    conversation prepared by Dulles the next day, the following exchange occurred:
                        <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Macmillan took me aside, and said he wished to express his deep
                            appreciation for our help on the Cyprus matter. He said that he had sent
                            me a written message, but he wanted to reinforce it personally. I said
                            that it had been a difficult decision to take, but having taken it, we
                            went through with it in helping to get the votes necessary to succeed.
                            Otherwise, the British would have been defeated. Macmillan said he was
                            well aware of that fact. He went on to say he was determined to carry
                            the thing through to a successful conclusion, and would make some
                            constructive proposals in the very near future.” (Department of State,
                            Secretary’s Memoranda of Conversation: Lot 64 D 199)</p>
                    </quote></p>
            </div>
